  <static seed="114" source="Library/Valdosta/APEX_Calculus/2.4/APEX_2.4_26.pg">
    <statement><p>Compute the derivative of the given function.</p>
<p><me>g(t) = -4 t^{4} e^t  - 6 \sin t \cos t</me></p>
<p><m>g'(t) =</m><fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/>.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-4t^{3}\mathopen{}\left(4+t\right)e^{t}+\left(-6\right)\cos^{2}\mathopen{}\left(t\right)-\left(-6\right)\sin^{2}\mathopen{}\left(t\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert> Solution:</alert> 



Use the Product Rule on each term.



<me>\begin{array}{rcl}
    g'(t) \amp =\amp  [(-4 t^{4})(e^t) + (-16 t^{3})(e^t)]
     - 6 [ (\sin t)(-\sin t) + (\cos t)(\cos t)] \\
    \amp \amp \\
    \amp =\amp  -4 t^{3}(4 + t)e^t  + 6 \sin^2 t  - 6 \cos^2 t
  \end{array}</me>


</solution>
